{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["(vv. 18–19) <b>And Pharaoh called, etc. Why, etc.; why, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did he double the question twice in an unusual manner? And perhaps it intends to say two levels in his astonishment on this matter: the first — it was fitting for you to be concerned for me, lest I not know that she is your wife and I take her; and furthermore, that you added to act by saying explicitly ״אֲחוֹתִי הִיא״." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it may be explained along the way of Rashi, of blessed memory: the reason Pharaoh said to him ״הִנֵּה אִשְׁתְּךָ קַח וָלֵךְ״, whereas Avimelech said to him ״הִנֵּה אַרְצִי לְפָנֶיךָ״ etc., is that Pharaoh, since his men were licentious and steeped in lust, was concerned lest they stumble in her and be punished — unlike Avimelech, whose men were not licentious, etc. And accordingly you will find that Avimelech said to him ״מָה רָאִיתָ כִּי עָשִׂיתָ״ etc., whereas Pharaoh did not, for he knew Avraham's reason — that he feared lest he be killed on her account. From this the verse is properly explained: since Pharaoh knew the reason, it would not be justified to say to him ״לָמָה אָמַרְתָּ אֲחוֹתִי הִיא״, but only the first question, ״לָמָה לֹא הִגַּדְתָּ לִי״ — specifically that she is your wife. For by this, even if you say ״אֲחוֹתִי הִיא״, you would have had protection from the people and would not have caused me harm by stumbling with her, for Pharaoh would certainly have held himself as one who would do nothing disgraceful once he knew she was a wife, and Avraham would not have suspected him. And the telling would have benefited Avraham in one of two ways: either Pharaoh would command men to guard him, as he in fact did shortly thereafter, or he would hasten to send him away. And he further says <b>לָמָה אָמַרְתָּ</b> etc., meaning: and if you will say, is it then a man's obligation that every man who brings his wife must go and tell Pharaoh that this is his wife? Therefore he said ״לָמָה אָמַרְתָּ״ etc. — and from the very reason that you said ״אֲחוֹתִי הִיא״ on account of her extraordinary beauty, it is therefore upon you in particular to inform." "<b>Or</b> it may say along this line: <b>לָמָה אָמַרְתָּ</b> etc. — and since you spoke, you should have been concerned for me that I would take her as a wife. True, had you not said anything, I would have been wary of her as a married woman; but since you said ״אֲחוֹתִי״, you should have been concerned for me that I would take her as a wife, and you should have told me in confidence. And Avraham did not reply at all, as he replied to Avimelech, because Avraham's reason was that he suspected Pharaoh himself, and it is not fitting to reply to that. Or perhaps it is for this reason that Pharaoh was concerned and hastened to send him away from his land, for otherwise he would have continually posted men over him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:07.376027425Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}